{"text": "Lesson two. Bottlenecks, the roofline, and the metrics that matter. Operational intensity is floating point work divided by bytes moved from main memory. Hardware gives you two peaks: peak compute, called pi in operations per 2nd, peak memory bandwidth, called beta in bytes per 2nd. Their ratio is the ridge point. It is the lowest intensity that can keep the compute units full. Below the ridge, you are memory bound. Performance follows bandwidth times intensity. At or above the ridge, you hit the flat compute ceiling. Prefill sits on or above the ridge. Decode at batch size one sits far below it. Here is the number people misremember. At batch size one in 16-bit floating point, a decode step does about two times the parameter count in floating point operations. That, too, is the multiply-accumulate, two operations per parameter. The same step moves about two times the parameter count in bytes, because each weight is two bytes. Those two factors of two are unrelated. They happen to cancel, so intensity is about one operation per byte. Eight-bit integers halve the bytes and double the intensity. Four-bit integers double it again. Modern accelerators have ridge points in the hundreds of operations per byte, so decode is orders of magnitude under the ceiling. Larger batches, fused kernels, and less traffic per step are how later pillars push decode toward the ridge. Four physical bottlenecks. Memory capacity limits how many users fit on one accelerator. Memory bandwidth limits how fast one user is served. A rough ceiling on tokens per 2nd is bandwidth divided by two times the parameter count in 16-bit. Compute throughput limits prefill and large batch decode, and it depends on the numeric format. Interconnect and numeric support limits sharding and splitting prefill from decode. Who owns them? Capacity is pillars one, three, and four. Bandwidth is pillars one, two, and four. Compute is pillars two and three. Interconnect is pillar four. Metrics: aggregate tokens per 2nd can hide a bad experience for one user. Goodput counts only tokens that meet a latency target. Time to 1st token is prefill plus queuing. Time per output token is decode, set by bandwidth and cache size. For speculation, track the mean number of accepted tokens per step. Speedup is about that length, minus the cost of checking the draft.", "language": "en", "duration": 149.16, "words": [{"text": "Lesson", "start": 0.14, "end": 0.44}, {"text": "two.", "start": 0.54, "end": 0.76}, {"text": "Bottlenecks,", "start": 1.541, "end": 2.161}, {"text": "the", "start": 2.301, "end": 2.381}, {"text": "roofline,", "start": 2.461, "end": 3.041}, {"text": "and", "start": 3.161, "end": 3.241}, {"text": "the", "start": 3.261, "end": 3.321}, {"text": "metrics", "start": 3.381, "end": 3.801}, {"text": "that", "start": 3.821, "end": 3.941}, {"text": "matter.", "start": 3.981, "end": 4.301}, {"text": "Operational", "start": 5.022, "end": 5.602}, {"text": "intensity", "start": 5.642, "end": 6.162}, {"text": "is", "start": 6.242, "end": 6.342}, {"text": "floating", "start": 6.422, "end": 6.782}, {"text": "point", "start": 6.822, "end": 7.062}, {"text": "work", "start": 7.122, "end": 7.322}, {"text": "divided", "start": 7.382, "end": 7.763}, {"text": "by", "start": 7.803, "end": 7.923}, {"text": "bytes", "start": 8.023, "end": 8.343}, {"text": "moved", "start": 8.423, "end": 8.723}, {"text": "from", "start": 8.783, "end": 8.923}, {"text": "main", "start": 8.983, "end": 9.183}, {"text": "memory.", "start": 9.243, "end": 9.583}, {"text": "Hardware", "start": 10.203, "end": 10.604}, {"text": "gives", "start": 10.644, "end": 10.824}, {"text": "you", "start": 10.864, "end": 10.944}, {"text": "two", "start": 11.024, "end": 11.184}, {"text": "peaks:", "start": 11.264, "end": 11.644}, {"text": "peak", "start": 12.124, "end": 12.304}, {"text": "compute,", "start": 12.364, "end": 12.884}, {"text": "called", "start": 13.064, "end": 13.284}, {"text": "pi", "start": 13.344, "end": 13.705}, {